#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# tan

#% text
# `tan(x)` gives the tangent of `x`, `sin(x)/cos(x)`, with `x` in radians.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# `tan(q*pi)` is exact for a rational `q` whose denominator is 1, 3, 4 or 6; at odd multiples of `pi/2` it has poles. Other exact arguments stay unevaluated.

#% item
# A minus sign is taken out: `tan(-x)` is `-tan(x)`. The period is `pi`.

#% item
# Machine numbers, balls and complex numbers give numerical values. `tan` is listable.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
tan(pi/4)
#% output
#= 1

#% cell
tan(pi/3)
#% output
#= 3^(1/2)

#% cell
tan(1.0)
#% output
#= 1.5574077246549023

#% cell
tan([0, pi/6, pi/4])
#% output
#= [0, (1/3)*3^(1/2), 1]

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# 30 digits:

#% cell
n(tan(1), 30)
#% output
#= 1.5574077246549022305069748074583602

#% text
# A complex argument:

#% cell
tan(1.0 + 1.0*im)
#% output
#= 0.2717525853195118 + 1.0839233273386946im

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The derivative:

#% cell
d(tan(x), x)
#% output
#= cos(x)^-2

#% text
# The inverse:

#% cell
atan(tan(0.5))
#% output
#= 0.5

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# The pole at `pi/2`:

#% cell
tan(pi/2)
#% output
#= tan((1/2)*pi)

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `sin`, `cos`, `atan`, `tanh`
